WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.039 --> 00:00:02.938
Mind Your Decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:07.560
Triangle ABC has an inscribed circle I.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.420
The inscribed circle is tangent to BC at point D.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.820
If the radius of the inscribed circle is 15,

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.940
BD = 26,

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.940
and DC = 25,

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.460
what is the area of triangle ABC?

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:29.960
Pause if you'd like to give this problem a try, and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.669
I'm first going to review the concepts used to solve this problem.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.699
These will be equal tangents to a circle,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.140
the area of a triangle from its in radius,

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.220
and Heron's formula for the area of a triangle.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.220
We'll get started with equal tangents to a circle.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.629
Consider circle P, and an exterior point A.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.169
Construct tangents AB and AC.

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:09.619
We want to show that AB = AC.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.570
To do that, we'll construct radii PB and PC.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.990
We then construct AP.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:23.419
It's obvious now that ABP and ACP are congruent right triangles.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.120
To be more explicit, notice that AP is a hypotenuse length W.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.700
Therefore, the remaining leg in the right triangle

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:35.000
will be equal to √(w^2-r^2), and

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.100
that's true in both of these right triangles.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.880
Therefore, AB = AC.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.700
Now, let's derive the area of a triangle from its inradius.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.680
In a triangle with sides ABC,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.320
consider the inscribed circle.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.710
Draw radii to each of the points of tangency.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.200
We can therefore partition the triangle into three smaller triangles.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:06.500
One triangle will be alongside A. This triangle will have an area of AR/2.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.220
This triangle alongside B will have an area of BR/2.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.869
Finally, the triangle alongside C has an area of CR/2.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:22.729
We can simplify this formula, and then factor out the r.

00:02:23.519 --> 00:02:28.040
We thus have r(perimeter)/2.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:33.619
The perimeter over two is known as the semi-perimeter, and often abbreviated as s.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.829
Therefore, the formula is rs.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.600
Finally, we have Heron's formula for the area of a triangle.

00:02:42.239 --> 00:02:44.239
It'll be equal to the following expression:

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:50.690
√(s(s - a)(s - b)(s - c))

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.000
Here, s again is the semi-perimeter.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.150
I'll now briefly give a proof of Heron's formula.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:06.000
By Al-Kashi's law of cosines, c^2 = a^2+b^2 - 2ab cos c.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.080
In this equation, we'll solve for the cosine of angle C.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:17.639
We'll then use the identity that sin C = √(1 - cos^2).

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:21.720
Substitute that in, and then simplify.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:28.410
We then use a formula that the area of a triangle = (1/2) (ab) sin C.

00:03:29.109 --> 00:03:31.169
Again, we substitute and simplify.

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:35.639
We're now going to use a lot of algebra to simplify this equation.

00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:40.048
First, we have a difference of squares so we can factor it as follows.

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:43.858
We're then going to factor each of these terms, and then

00:03:45.129 --> 00:03:49.319
We have another difference of squares, so we factor each of these quantities.

00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:52.349
We then simplify this formula.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.179
Now we're going to take that 1/4 and put it inside as 1/16.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:02.609
We can distribute this to each of the 4 terms, because 2^4 = 16.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:07.619
Finally, the semi-perimeter = (a+b+c)/2.

00:04:08.079 --> 00:04:12.059
So, the first term will be s, the second term will be s-c,

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:16.349
the third term will be s-a, and the last term will be s-b.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:20.965
We substitute those in, and then we slightly rearrange this formula,

00:04:20.965 --> 00:04:24.700
and we get exactly Heron's formula. We've proven it.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.580
So now, let's use each of these concepts to solve the problem.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.380
First, equal tangents.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:38.169
BD=BF. Therefore, BF = 26.

00:04:38.869 --> 00:04:44.018
Then, CD and CE are equal tangents. Therefore, CE = 25.

00:04:45.229 --> 00:04:49.389
Here, if AF = x,AE = x.

00:04:50.899 --> 00:04:56.828
Consequently, side AB = 26+x,  AC = 25+x,

00:04:57.469 --> 00:05:00.789
and BC = 26+25 = 51.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.660
We will now calculate the area of triangle ABC in two different ways.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:07.760
First, we'll calculate its area from the inradius.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.800
We first calculate the semi-perimeter.

00:05:11.119 --> 00:05:17.289
This will be the perimeter/2. This simplifies to 51+x.

00:05:18.559 --> 00:05:25.928
The area will be rs, which will be equal to 15(51 + x).

00:05:27.439 --> 00:05:29.439
We'll put this equation to the side.

00:05:30.049 --> 00:05:32.049
We then use Heron's formula.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:36.480
So, we have the formula, we've calculated the semi-perimeter,

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:39.900
and we substitute in to get the area of the triangle in this form.

00:05:40.429 --> 00:05:44.738
We thus have two equations for the area of the triangle, which we set equal to each other.

00:05:45.469 --> 00:05:49.838
We now systematically solve this equation to solve for the variable x.

00:05:50.689 --> 00:05:54.099
This is basic algebra, so I'll just go through it quickly.

00:05:55.039 --> 00:05:59.468
We then end up with x = 27, but we're not trying to solve for x;

00:05:59.469 --> 00:06:01.469
we're trying to solve for the area of the triangle,

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:08.180
So we go back to the formula for the area and then substitute, and we get the answer of 1,170.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.900
I love [that] this problem involves so many different concepts from geometry.

00:06:14.389 --> 00:06:18.160
Thanks for making Mind Your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.269
As always, thanks for watching and thanks for your support.

